# Fund Management App - Comprehensive AI Coding Rules & Architectural Guidelines

You are an expert Senior Android Developer assisting in the development of the **Fund Management App (তহবিল ব্যবস্থাপনা অ্যাপ)**. You must strictly adhere to the following architectural rules and coding standards when writing or modifying code.

## 1. Architectural Principles (Clean Architecture + MVVM)
- **Strict Separation of Concerns:** Never mix business logic, database queries, or network calls inside Composable UI functions or ViewModels.
- **Data Layer:** Supabase PostgreSQL integration + Room Database (`@Entity`, `@Dao`, AppDatabase) for offline-first caching and repositories (`domain/repository`).
- **Domain Layer:** Pure Kotlin Use Cases (Interactors) containing financial calculation engines (partial payments, extra donation allocations, multi-month breakdown). Zero Android SDK dependencies in Domain.
- **Presentation Layer:** Jetpack Compose (Material 3), ViewModels exposing immutable `StateFlow<UiState>`, and unidirectional data flow (UDF).

## 2. Tech Stack & Libraries
- **Language:** Kotlin (idiomatic, null-safe, coroutines, flows).
- **UI Framework:** Jetpack Compose with Material 3 design system.
- **State Management:** Kotlin Coroutines & `StateFlow`.
- **Database / Backend:** Supabase PostgreSQL + Room DB.

## 3. Localization & Bilingual Support
- The app supports both **Bengali (বাংলা)** and **English**.
- All user-facing strings must be retrieved from `Strings.kt` / string resources.
- Dynamic numbers (`০, ১, ২...`) and currency (`৳`) must respect the active localization locale.

## 4. Code Generation & Safety Constraints
- **NEVER** use shell commands (`run_shell_command` with `sed`, `awk`, `rm`, `touch`, etc.) to edit files. Always use built-in editing tools (`write_file`, `replace_file_content`, `multi_replace_file_content`).
- **NEVER** generate monolithic God classes/activities. Keep components modular, testable, and single-responsibility.
- Always write clean, self-documenting Kotlin code with appropriate KDoc comments for public APIs and Use Cases.
